  "narrative_body": "Russian electronic warfare redirected two Ukrainian drones into Latvian airspace on May 7, and one struck oil storage tanks near Rēzekne — the first time weapons have caused physical damage on Latvian territory in the current conflict era. Three days later, the incident had cost the Defence Minister his job and left the governing coalition on the edge of collapse.\n\nThe drones entered from Russian territory. General Kaspars Pudāns, the National Armed Forces commander, confirmed publicly that detection technology had failed. A second air-threat warning went out the following night across eastern municipalities, with NATO fighters scrambled. Andrii Sybiha, Ukraine's foreign minister, subsequently confirmed that Russian electronic warfare had deliberately diverted the drones from their targets inside Russia into Latvian airspace — transforming what might have been treated as a navigation accident into documented Russian aggression against NATO territory. Mr Sybiha told Latvia's foreign minister, Baiba Braže, that Ukraine was ready to work with the Baltic states and Finland to prevent further incidents, including by deploying Ukrainian specialists.\n\nEvika Siliņa, the prime minister, did not wait. On May 10, she demanded Defence Minister Andris Sprūds's resignation via social media, citing slow counter-drone deployment and late civilian alerts. Mr Sprūds resigned — but not quietly. At a press conference he said he was acting \"to protect Latvia's army from divisive political campaigning,\" accused New Unity of running a \"systematic campaign\" against the Progressives because the smaller party refused to \"toe the line,\" and said the governing party had \"wilted under pressure from various other parties\" ahead of elections. He said he would remain in the Saeima.\n\nThe Progressives hold 10 seats in the 53-seat coalition. Their departure would drop the government to 43 seats, below the 51 needed for a majority, with no viable replacement partner in reach. The Green-Farmers Union said it would stay; the National Alliance had already prepared its own resignation demand for Mr Sprūds before Ms Siliņa moved first. The Progressives formally announced they would decide the following week whether to remain. Because no viable alternative majority exists, the likeliest outcome is that they extract concessions and stay — but the relationship between the two leading coalition partners is now openly hostile, and Mr Sprūds's public accusations make it hard to paper over.\n\nMs Siliņa filled the vacancy immediately, naming Colonel Raivis Melnis — previously the Defence Ministry's representative in Ukraine — as the new minister. The appointment was made without consulting the Progressives. Colonel Melnis brings direct operational experience from Ukraine, but his installation by social media announcement, outside normal process, gave Ms Siliņa's critics a ready argument: that she used the Rēzekne incident to consolidate New Unity's grip on the Defence Ministry five months before the October 3 election.\n\nThe week brought further pressure on the governing coalition's primary electoral challenger. KNAB, the Corruption Prevention Bureau, opened a review into a campaign operation run by Ainārs Šlesers — founder of Latvia First — that has spent roughly €275,000 promoting him as \"Premier Šlesers\" through Riga billboards, bus stop posters, digital screens, and radio jingles. KNAB is investigating whether the spending, channeled through an association called \"Latvija pirmā,\" constitutes an illegal donation to Latvia First, which as a party cannot legally receive money from a legal entity. KNAB's division chief publicly expressed \"surprise\" at Mr Šlesers's claim that he had cleared his approach with the bureau in advance. If a violation is found, Latvia First faces penalties, repayment of the illegal funds, and potential loss of all state financing — a severe blow before October. At the same moment, Latvia First's pension referendum is collecting signatures: the Central Election Commission registered the initiative, which seeks roughly 150,000 names to force parliamentary consideration of a law letting pension second-pillar participants withdraw accumulated savings before retirement age. The Finance Ministry, the Bank of Latvia, and financial sector bodies have all opposed it. The referendum is the electoral instrument; KNAB is the obstacle.\n\nAgainst the domestic turbulence, Edgars Rinkēvičs, the president, traveled to Yerevan on May 7, meeting his Armenian counterpart Vahagn Khachaturyan and Prime Minister Nikol Pashinyan. A joint statement called for cooperation to disrupt EU sanctions circumvention — directly relevant given Latvia's role as a sanctions-enforcement partner. Mr Rinkēvičs's presence carried additional weight: the same day, Vladimir Putin warned Armenia that EU integration could produce consequences \"like Ukraine.\" The same week, European Commissioner Valdis Dombrovskis presented Ms Siliņa with confirmation of Latvia's fourth EU Recovery Fund tranche — €371.2 million, bringing total receipts to roughly €1.5 billion, or 75% of the full €1.97 billion allocation. Forty-one plan milestones fulfilled and verified. The institutions delivered while the politicians fought.\n\nThe economic picture darkened independently. Swedbank's chief economist for Latvia, Līva Zorgenfreija, cut the bank's 2026 GDP growth forecast from 2.3% to 1.9% and raised its average annual inflation forecast from 2.8% to 4.2%, citing the Hormuz Strait closure as the primary driver. Energy prices, particularly diesel and aviation fuel, have risen sharply. In a worst case — prolonged closure — inflation could reach 8% and growth could slow to 1%. The European Central Bank is expected to raise rates from 2% to 2.5% in response to eurozone-wide pressure. Latvia's defence spending is locked at nearly 5% of GDP. If growth slips, the fiscal choices narrow further.\n\nOne thing went unreported this week that deserves attention. Latvia's election IT vendor transition — required after EU investigators found national security risks in the original procurement — needs to succeed within roughly five months for credible October 3 elections. The ministry overseeing that transition is a coalition portfolio. If the Progressives exit, ministerial assignments shift and management of the transition is disrupted at the moment it needs the opposite. No reporting on progress appeared this week. The silence is not reassuring.",
